In article <6j9ukg$c3l$1@nnrp1.dejanews.com>, c-willden@mail.dec.com wrote:

>I would like an ascii dump of a binary file. I do
>not need to maintain the integrity of the file, I just want to be able to read
>it.  I did strings, but I'm not sure that I got the entire file.  Also, I
>don't want all the numerical output that vmses dump command gives me (I don't
>use vms very much, so maybe the dump command would have given me what I
>wanted.)

Sounds like you want something cheap and dirty.  Try

    tr -c '[:print:]' '.' <binary_file >ascii_dump

The -c option tells 'tr' to select the characters that do not match the
first string.  The first string '[:print:]' specifies all printable
characters, so all non-printable characters will be selected.  The second
string tells 'tr' to replace selected characters with a period (you may use
any replacement character you like - such as a space ' ').  'tr' reads
stdin and writes to stdout so you have to use I/O redirection to get it to
read your binary file.

Give it a try.  If it is not what you want them maybe reading the man page
on 'tr' will give you some other ideas.

-- 
